Figure 12 displays "Idealized characters used in seismic stratigraphy interpretation" (b), which provided the motivation for later developments in geometric attributes, including volumetric dip and azimuth, reflector parallelism, continuity, and unconformity indicators. The text does not explicitly state which seismic attributes are shown specifically for gas in clastic sediments in Figure 12(b); it describes the idealized characters used in seismic stratigraphy interpretation that motivated later geometric attributes. Source: gsgpy_70_5_3SO.pdf — 9.

Figure 12 (page 9) of file gsgpy_70_5_3SO.pdf shows:
{IMAGE:12}
Seismic attributes such as reﬂection characters, including onlap, ofﬂap, and other morphological patterns, are shown for the gas in clastic sediments example in figure 12B.

The major technological advancement associated with the early growth of seismic attributes in the 1960s is digital recording of seismic data, which occurred around 1960 according to the timeline. Figure 2 on page 5 shows an example of a computer-generated seismic attribute from 1962, which involved a device built to crosscorrelate seismic traces recorded on analog magnetic tape.

